  "scientific_limits": {
    "en": "[\"Scale and shape are from different published material; explicitly disclose genus-level interpretation, not single-specimen measured replica.\", \"No direct basal photograph of the same FigB/C fossil; base and umbilical depth are conservatively inferred from M’Coy description. A basal QA render is required to expose this interpretation.\", \"Fine apertural lip and missing surface cannot be restored as pristine anatomy. Fossil termination remains mineral-filled.\", \"Euomphalus sometimes treated historically as subgenus of Straparollus; target is type species E.pentangulatus, not substituted S.northi. No soft anatomy or higher phylogenetic claims.\"] {\"unit\": \"metre\", \"diameter\": 0.0508, \"height\": 0.01905, \"last_whorl_thickness\": 0.01693, \"basis\": \"English inch25.4mm; line assumed1/12inch. Rounded working dimensions51mm wide,19mm high,17mm lastwhorl. Historical species-description values, not a calibrated individual reconstruction.\", \"tolerance\": \"Approximately +/-10% simplification; avoid false precision. In-plane second diameter about45–51mm inferred from photographs, not independently measured.\"}",
    "zh": "原创低多边形研究；形态、尺度及不确定性严格以本次已核参考为界。原始限定：[\"Scale and shape are from different published material; explicitly disclose genus-level interpretation, not single-specimen measured replica.\", \"No direct basal photograph of the same FigB/C fossil; base and umbilical depth are conservatively inferred from M’Coy description. A basal QA render is required to expose this interpretation.\", \"Fine apertural lip and missing surface cannot be restored as pristine anatomy. Fossil termination remains mineral-filled.\", \"Euomphalus sometimes treated historically as subgenus of Straparollus; target is type species E.pentangulatus, not substituted S.northi. No soft anatomy or higher phylogenetic claims.\"] {\"unit\": \"metre\", \"diameter\": 0.0508, \"height\": 0.01905, \"last_whorl_thickness\": 0.01693, \"basis\": \"English inch25.4mm; line assumed1/12inch. Rounded working dimensions51mm wide,19mm high,17mm lastwhorl. Historical species-description values, not a calibrated individual reconstruction.\", \"tolerance\": \"Approximately +/-10% simplification; avoid false precision. In-plane second diameter about45–51mm inferred from photographs, not independently measured.\"}"
  },
